WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.535
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.535 --> 00:00:05.460
we help a semi-aquatic, egg
laying, mammal-based calculator

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:09.210
unravel its dependency problem,
and then we do nothing.

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:11.514
Stay tuned for more.

00:00:11.514 --> 00:00:13.680
I was grabbing lunch last
week with a good developer

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.180
friend of mine, and
the conversation

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:17.430
turned to lamentations around
the limits of Google.com

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:18.891
as a calculator.

00:00:18.891 --> 00:00:21.390
I mean, frankly, it's one of
the more accessible calculators

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:24.265
I've ever used, but I end
up hitting the limits of it

00:00:24.265 --> 00:00:24.890
pretty quickly.

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:27.630
It won't even compute
the factorial of 256.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:29.460
My friend then revealed
that she created

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:33.600
her own version of Google.com
calculator called Platy-Calc.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.910
However, her version
ran on App Engine,

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:37.950
and evaluated the
submitted math expressions

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:40.230
using the Python
runtime, which can easily

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:42.510
calculate the factorial of 256.

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:44.700
To me, this was
immediately brilliant,

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:47.940
and I started using Platy-Calc
as my primary web-based

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:48.870
calculator.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:51.540
However, I started noticing that
the first request of the day

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:54.540
was always a little slower
than subsequent requests.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:56.970
Seeing as I've been talking
about cold boot time a lot

00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:58.530
lately, I reached
out to my friend

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:01.260
and asked to do some
profiling on the application.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:03.420
A first look at Platy-Calc
showed pretty much

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:06.540
what I was expecting-- higher
than normal cold boot time.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:08.830
Now, the code for this
app was really simple.

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:11.280
I mean, there's no global
variables, no locking issues,

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.650
and no NDB fetches
in the startup path.

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:15.660
The only thing that
was occurring globally

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:17.670
during boot time was
that it was importing

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:20.310
some complex numeric
processing libraries.

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:22.560
Given the fact that we've
already timed a simple Hello

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.170
World app and seen what
startup times look like,

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.510
these imports look
like a culprit.

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:29.580
Just as a test, we
removed those imports

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:31.830
and retimed the
startup process, just

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:35.340
to see if App Engine cold boot
time had suddenly regressed,

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:37.890
or if there was some
other issue going on.

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:39.930
Thankfully, we
weren't going crazy,

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:42.060
as our timing showed pretty
much the same startup

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:44.100
behavior as I had seen before.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.870
At this point,
process of elimination

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:48.120
was clearly porting
to the imports

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:50.130
as the source of the
startup time issues.

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:52.530
I mean, there was nothing
else going on in the app.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:54.990
However, it felt wrong to
simply discard them outright.

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:56.460
We needed more information.

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:58.920
As such, we put together
a small code snippet

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:02.640
that timed how long it took for
each of these imports to occur.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:05.470
Our profiling showed us
two clear data points.

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:07.290
Number one, the
summation of imports

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:10.800
is easily adding about 1.25
seconds to cold boot time

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:11.730
to the application.

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:13.920
And number two, obviously
some of the imports

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:15.900
were taking longer than others.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:18.450
Now, number two is really
the interesting point here,

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:20.340
and it's worth taking
a second to discuss.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:23.070
See, during cold boot
time, your application

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:25.860
will be busy scanning and
importing code dependencies.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:28.170
The longer it takes
to do this, the longer

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:30.970
it will take before your first
line of code is executed.

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:33.240
Now, some languages can
optimize this process

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:34.870
to be exceptionally fast.

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:36.780
Other languages may
be slower at it,

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:38.700
but they provide
more flexibility.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:40.290
And to be fair,
most of the time,

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:43.080
a standard application
importing a few modules

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:44.760
is less than impactful.

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:47.760
However, when a third party
library gets big enough,

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:49.620
we start to see
them do weird things

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:52.860
with import schematics that can
often mess up your cold boot

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:53.700
time.

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:57.000
A simple Google search revealed
that some other developers

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:58.532
saw similar import time issues.

00:02:58.532 --> 00:03:00.240
So it was clear that
this was the problem

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:01.650
we were running into.

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:04.230
Given the fact that imports
were required for our operation,

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:07.110
our hands were a bit tied with
respect to an elegant solution.

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:10.770
Nonetheless, we came up with a
few ideas to address the issue.

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:13.170
Number one, use a
warm up request.

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:15.480
When the website is
loading from static assets,

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:18.030
send a quick ping to the
calculator service, which

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:19.710
will import the
required modules,

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:22.740
rather than waiting for the
calculate button to be pressed.

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:25.260
Number two, lazy
load our imports.

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:27.450
Python supports this
operation by allowing

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:30.150
global versus local
importing of modules.

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:33.240
So we could move all of the
imports to be function local.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:34.890
So if you just want
to do 2 plus 2,

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:38.130
you don't have to wait to
import the entire SciPy module.

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:40.610
Only when we detect
some specific keyword,

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:42.750
we'd import those
specific things.

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:45.470
And number three, prune
the dependency tree.

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:47.850
We've had a lot of Stack
Overflow posts and tutorials

00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:50.310
about how to manually prune
the dependency tree by either

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:52.620
explicitly importing
sub portions of it

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:55.620
or by doing some fun hacks that
included mirroring the module

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:57.690
and removing the
things we didn't need.

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:01.230
But given all that, we
decided to do nothing.

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:04.410
Yep, Platy-Calc is not a
huge million QPS service.

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:06.660
It's just a small
application used

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:08.760
by a few friends who
really need to compute

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:10.650
large numbers from a
command line interface

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:12.360
for some crazy reason.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:14.370
So waiting an extra
second every few days

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:16.994
for a query that wasn't
big of a deal in our case,

00:04:16.994 --> 00:04:18.660
we decided that's the
right thing to do.

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:19.320
Oh, by the way.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:23.070
If you're looking for what the
factorial of 256 actually is,

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:23.910
there you go.

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:25.111
Thanks, Platy-Calc.

00:04:25.111 --> 00:04:27.360
Now, as much as I'd like to
beat the performance drum,

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:29.490
truth is that
sometimes the result

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:32.100
on the end user for a
particular perf improvement

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:34.830
just isn't justified given
the work needed to fix it.

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:37.860
So remember, every
millisecond counts,

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:42.210
except in the few times
where it kind of doesn't.

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:45.860
[MUSIC PLAYING]

